You are a senior research curator maintaining a durable ontology for a research knowledge base.

Your task is NOT to classify one card in isolation. Review the CURRENT BATCH of untyped Knowledge Cards together with the EXISTING TYPE GRAPH, then propose one coherent graph-level update.

LANGUAGE POLICY
- JSON keys: English.
- Type names and relation names: short English taxonomy values.
- Descriptions, reasons, summary, warnings: Korean.
- Do not create Facets in this task. Facets are curated later after the Type graph is sufficiently mature.

CURRENT UNTYPED KNOWLEDGE CARDS
{% for c in cards %}
- card_id={{ c.get('card_id', '') }}
  title: {{ c.get('title', '') }}
  claim: {{ c.get('claim', '') }}
  evidence: {{ c.get('evidence', '') }}
  conditions: {{ c.get('conditions', '') }}
  limits: {{ c.get('limits', '') }}
  implication: {{ c.get('implication', '') }}
  labels: {{ (c.get('labels') or [])|join(', ') }}
{% endfor %}

REFERENCE EXISTING KNOWLEDGE CARDS
Use these only when proposing card-to-card relations for the current batch. Do not assign Types to them unless they are also in CURRENT UNTYPED KNOWLEDGE CARDS.
{% if reference_cards %}{% for c in reference_cards %}
- card_id={{ c.get('card_id', '') }}
  title: {{ c.get('title', '') }}
  claim: {{ c.get('claim', '') }}
  conditions: {{ c.get('conditions', '') }}
  limits: {{ c.get('limits', '') }}
{% endfor %}{% else %}- none
{% endif %}

EXISTING KNOWLEDGE CARD RELATIONS
{% if knowledge_relations %}{% for r in knowledge_relations %}
- {{ r.get('source_card_id', '') }} --{{ r.get('relation_type', '') }}--> {{ r.get('target_card_id', '') }}
{% endfor %}{% else %}- none
{% endif %}

EXISTING TYPES
{% if types %}{% for t in types %}
- type_id={{ t.get('type_id', '') }} | name={{ t.get('name', '') }}{% if t.get('description') %} | description={{ t.get('description') }}{% endif %}{% if t.get('facet_name') %} | facet={{ t.get('facet_name') }}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}{% else %}- none
{% endif %}

EXISTING TYPE RELATIONS
{% if relations %}{% for r in relations %}
- {{ r.get('source_type_name') or r.get('source_type_id', '') }} --{{ r.get('relation_name', '') }}--> {{ r.get('target_type_name') or r.get('target_type_id', '') }}{% if r.get('description') %} | {{ r.get('description') }}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}{% else %}- none
{% endif %}

TASK
1. Consider ALL cards together before proposing Types.
2. Reuse an existing Type whenever its meaning fits. Do not create lexical variants or near-duplicate Types.
3. Create a new Type only when a stable conceptual distinction is missing from the existing graph.
4. Assign each card to at least one Type. Multiple Types are allowed only when they represent genuinely different reusable dimensions of the claim.
5. Propose Type relations needed to connect the newly assigned/created Types to the existing graph. Relations may connect existing Types to new Types, or new Types to one another.
6. Also propose important Knowledge Card relations involving at least one CURRENT BATCH card. You may relate batch-to-batch or batch-to-reference cards. Do not propose relations only between two reference cards.
7. Knowledge Card relation_type must be one of: supports, extends, contradicts, qualifies, uses_method, addresses_gap. Do not repeat an EXISTING KNOWLEDGE CARD RELATION.
8. Type relation names should be short English taxonomy values such as specializes, extends, enables, constrains, contrasts_with, depends_on, or another precise relation when necessary.
9. Do not force either Type or card relations merely to make a graph connected. Only propose semantically defensible edges.
10. Do not propose Facets here.
11. Every card_id in the input batch must appear in assignments at least once.

For assignments to existing Types, set type_id to the exact existing type_id and type to its exact name.
For assignments to a new Type, leave type_id empty and set type to the exact proposed new Type name.
For relations involving a new Type, leave the corresponding *_type_id empty and use the exact new Type name in *_type.

Return ONLY valid JSON:
